00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.000
When you look out at a pond, you are rarely&nbsp;
ever staring at just a body of water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.720
some sort of liquid mass separate from the trees&nbsp;
and vegetation around it. The pond itself is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:41.120
usually full of life, sometimes as large as&nbsp;
a fish, sometimes as small as a bacterium.&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:48.720
But sometimes those tinier organisms come together&nbsp;
to form large colonies that coat the sediment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:55.600
in and around the pond. Like these beggiatoa,&nbsp;
which might look like bits of string unfurling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.920
underneath the microscope, but are actually&nbsp;
hundreds of individual bacteria that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:08.240
attached to each other to create a filament.
Beggiatoa are sulfur bacteria, and you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.880
see the sulfur they’ve all stored&nbsp;
shining as the filament glides past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.320
glittering like a diamond-studded bracelet.
And just as the bacteria gather to form a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:24.480
filament, the filaments gather to form a&nbsp;
mat, weaving together tens of thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:31.920
beggiatoa into a white carpet that lines surfaces&nbsp;
found in marine and freshwater habitats alike.&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:39.920
And that is great news for James, our master of&nbsp;
microscopes, because one of his sampling ponds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:45.840
happens to have some of these beggiatoa carpets,&nbsp;
making it very easy for him to just collect a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.280
from the mat to check out under the microscope.
Or at least, it used to be easy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:58.880
Recently, for some reason, the pond hasn’t&nbsp;
yielded quite as much beggiatoa as it used to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.000
In fact, it’s barely produced any.&nbsp;
We don’t know quite what happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.160
but James suspects a new resident of the pond:&nbsp;
a family of beavers that could have potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.440
changed some of the chemical composition,&nbsp;
making it less habitable for the beggiatoa.&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:22.320
But in its place, another bacteria has come&nbsp;
to take over the thick biofilms of the pond:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.480
filamentous cyanobacteria. And while there’s&nbsp;
plenty to distinguish one from the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.160
there’s one very important trait&nbsp;
that unites them and makes them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:40.720
similarly mysterious: it is in the way they move.
Maybe you’ve picked up on it already. The long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:47.360
lengths of beggiatoa and cyanobacteria gliding&nbsp;
across the slide. There are only a few groups of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.200
bacteria that do this kind of gliding, but they’re&nbsp;
found across a plethora of environments, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:03:00.320
ponds, soil, and, surprise, in our own mouths.
Those fast-moving bacteria are largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.720
moving with the aid of flagella,&nbsp;
a helical structure powered by ATP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:10.800
that propels the bacteria in different directions.&nbsp;
But gliding does not involve those flagella.&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.680
Gliding involves something much more slick&nbsp;
than flagella: complex polysaccharides, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:24.800
scientists call by a much cooler name, slime.
A thread gliding across slime sounds more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.200
like an arts and crafts project gone wrong&nbsp;
than a complex form of motion that shapes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:34.720
entire ecosystems. But of course, that&nbsp;
is exactly what gliding turns out to be.&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.120
To appreciate the importance of this&nbsp;
movement, let’s continue with cyanobacteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.840
which have been photosynthesizing and&nbsp;
oxygenating our world for billions of years.&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:52.160
They are small, but the impact they are&nbsp;
able to have on the world around them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:57.600
is huge. And all this without the&nbsp;
aid of flagella to move them around.&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.360
Of course, it would be a massive challenge for&nbsp;
cyanobacteria to survive if they couldn’t move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.240
anywhere. They are photosynthetic organisms,&nbsp;
after all, so even if they’re not hunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:14.880
down prey, they are on the hunt for sunlight.
Fortunately, they already have the tools in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.480
place to sense sunlight. The pigments they use&nbsp;
to perform photosynthesis can also—when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:27.200
detect light— set off the reactions that drive the&nbsp;
cyanobacteria in the right direction. But not all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:33.840
light is the same: some species of cyanobacteria,&nbsp;
for example, will glide away from blue light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:39.440
while others ignore color and instead gauge&nbsp;
their response based on the intensity of light.&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.360
These responses will vary with the&nbsp;
species, as will the way they glide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:49.760
Some can bend, others can rotate around the axis&nbsp;
of their filament. But the end result is the same:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:55.840
a slow, but directed movement in search&nbsp;
of light that will one day be food.&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:03.200
But these filamentous cyanobacteria have hit on&nbsp;
more than just a steady, graceful path towards the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:10.240
light. They are collective organisms, gathering&nbsp;
into filaments and mats. They can even aggregate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.880
into toxic blooms that can span hundreds of square&nbsp;
miles, suffocating or poisoning the unfortunate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:21.440
organisms that come in contact with them.
And one of the challenges of forming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.920
these kinds of collections is that it&nbsp;
requires diverting resources towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:32.160
making the extracellular structures that keep the&nbsp;
cells together. And that can come at the expense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:38.160
of dividing and making more cyanobacteria.
So if there are disadvantages, there must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:44.560
be an advantage, and there’s a big one. By working&nbsp;
together, these colonies can allow the individuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:51.600
to better pursue a collective goal. And gliding&nbsp;
happens to just be one of those collective goals.&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.280
In the case of the model cyanobacteria species&nbsp;
Synechocystis, researchers have found that while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.920
individuals of the species can navigate in&nbsp;
response to light, they’re better able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.960
orient themselves to light when they’re attached&nbsp;
to each other. It’s like they go from stumbling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.640
individuals to a well-choreographed dance line.
And the mathematical models suggest that even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.120
if a fraction of this colony can’t respond&nbsp;
to light, the overall movement around them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:23.600
will stay the same—taking everyone towards light&nbsp;
and towards photosynthesis and continued life.&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.320
So this seemingly simple movement&nbsp;
brings complexity to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.520
seemingly simple organisms. And scientists&nbsp;
have been trying to decipher the mechanisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:40.000
that drive gliding for many decades now.
We mentioned earlier that where there’s gliding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:47.600
there’s also slime. But we don’t know what that&nbsp;
slime is actually doing. There have been theories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.920
of course. In one potential mechanism, the gliding&nbsp;
cyanobacteria oozes slime to power it along the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:59.600
surface. In another, waves travel down the length&nbsp;
of the filament to push it along the surface.&nbsp;

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:04.640
But even as scientists have identified more&nbsp;
of the components driving the movement of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:11.440
filamentous cyanobacteria, the process—or even&nbsp;
whether there is just one process—remains unclear.&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.440
It seems like it should be so much more obvious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:20.960
doesn’t it? That there should be an easy&nbsp;
answer. We know so much about flagella,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:25.360
and our own system of locomotion with&nbsp;
leg, well, that seems pretty obvious.&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:31.120
But then, maybe that’s what makes this so&nbsp;
beautiful and infuriatingly difficult to explain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:35.600
despite how long we’ve been&nbsp;
working to explain it. For now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:43.840
at least, it is a movement without an obvious&nbsp;
metaphor. one day, we’ll think of something.&nbsp;

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:53.520
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as we&nbsp;
glide through the unseen world that surrounds us&nbsp;

